syntax: parse declaration arguments with invalid names as words

Bash decides whether a declaration argument such as `export a-b=1` is an
assignment by checking the name when it parses it, and treats an invalid
name as an ordinary word: bash -n accepts it and the builtin fails at run
time with "not a valid identifier". The parser rejected the script with
"invalid var name", so tools lost everything after a typo like
`local 1st=a` or a template placeholder like

	export FLAGS_SET_@suffix@=1

Such an argument now becomes a naked Assign holding the word, like any
other non-assignment argument, so tools can point at the bad name.

Reproducer, before this change: `declare 0=x` with LangBash failed with
"invalid var name".

